  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 855 PM MST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nMILD AND DRY CONDITIONS THROUGH THANKSGIVING:\n\nA fast moving ridge continues to build over our area. Wednesday will \nlook similar to today with generally mild and dry conditions \nexpected. Patchy fog may develop again Wednesday morning in the \nlower valleys due to clear skies, light winds, and high relative \nhumidity from our recent system. Highest confidence would be in \nsouthern Colorado with potential for fog formation in the Uinta \nBasin. The ridge shifts eastward Wednesday night into Thursday. As \nthis occurs, a new ridge will build over Southern California and \ncreate a split flow pattern for Thanksgiving. This leads to our \nregion being sandwiched between a cutoff low to our south while \nanother powerful system develops to our north and east. A \ncontinuation of dry conditions with above-normal temperatures is \nexpected for Thanksgiving as both systems move away from our CWA. \n\n\nANOTHER SYSTEM EXPECTED AFTER THANKSGIVING:\n\nOn Friday and into Saturday, the ridge over Southern California \nbreaks down which will bring northwesterly flow. Confidence is high \non a system moving through within this time frame but there are \nvarying solutions and impacts. The GFS is showing a 250mb jet \nmax setting up over Wyoming and a weaker shortwave with lesser \nsnowfall totals primarily confined to the Park Range. The Euro \nis showing a stronger jet max setting up further south over \nwestern Colorado and eastern Utah with a more amplified, \nstronger trough. This stronger scenario would lead to higher \nsnowfall totals with impacts potentially extending as far south \nas the I-70 corridor. Due to these differing scenarios, \nconfidence in impacts and exact amounts remains low. Confidence \nis highest over the Park Range with lowering confidence to the\nsouth. Anyone traveling late this week and this weekend should \nprepare for potentially difficult conditions. Looking ahead, \nthere are some indications of another system impacting the \nregion early next week but guidance diverges on specific \nscenarios.",
